<title>GERMANY: Buba's Koebnick says sustainability crucial factor.</title>
<headline>Buba's Koebnick says sustainability crucial factor.</headline>
<dateline>HOMBURG, Germany 1996-11-21</dateline>
<text>
<p>Bundesbank council member Hans-Juergen Koebnick said sustainability was the most important factor for countries wanting to take part in European's monetary union.</p>
<p>Speaking in Homburg in southwestern Germany, Koebnick also said he was optimistic Germany would fulfill the Maastricht criteria on budget deficits in 1997.</p>
<p>&quot;The deciding factor will be sustainability,&quot; Koebnick said. &quot;It will not be a question of (a deficit of) 3.1 percent or 3.0 (percent).&quot;</p>
<p>Koebnick also was confident Germany's budget deficit would be reduced below the maximum 3.0 percent level in 1997 &quot;because there have been moves towards stability&quot; in Germany.</p>
<p>He was referring to Finance Minister Theo Waigel's tough 1997 budget cuts that call for a deficit no greater than 2.5 percent of GDP.</p>
<p>-- Bonn newsroom 49 228 260 970</p>
